-- postpartum week 43: Eating together, now that your baby eats too (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   43,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating together, now that your baby eats too',
   '"Your meal deserves a place at the table too — even when it gets interrupted."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the practical and emotional shift that happens once feeding your baby becomes part of your own mealtimes — and making sure that shift doesn''t quietly push your own eating to the side. By Postpartum Week 43, many families have already spent several months navigating complementary feeding, while others may be following a different timeline for their own reasons. This week doesn''t set any part of the baby''s diet, texture or feeding schedule — that''s between you and your baby''s paediatric or feeding team. Its only purpose is to stop the work of feeding the baby from repeatedly displacing your own meal. Feeding two people at the same table, on two different timelines, is its own kind of work — it''s easy for your own meal to become an afterthought: eaten cold, standing up, or skipped while you focus on someone else''s plate.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: family meals you share with your baby can usually fit within your existing plan — focus on your own portions and timing rather than mirroring your baby''s small, frequent meals. Ask your treating team if irregular mealtimes around your baby''s feeding schedule affect your glucose readings. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: avoid that food completely when preparing or sharing meals, and keep a safe alternative on hand for yourself.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa, uttapam, chilla or adai is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao, biryani or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you''re breastfeeding or follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Your meal remains a real meal", "notes": "Eating alongside your baby can easily turn into bites taken standing up, or whatever''s left once your baby''s plate is done. Aim to make enough food available for yourself, even if you eat it in stages. Sitting down may feel better when possible, but an interrupted meal still counts. If you cannot finish, make a practical plan to return to it or have something else.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-lentil adai (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with vegetable appam (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Savoury bowl of millet (1 cup cooked), paneer (75g) and vegetables", "lunch_b": "Chicken-chana pulao (100g chicken, 1/2 cup chana) with cooked vegetables and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit with unsweetened yoghurt (150g) and a tablespoon of seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted soy-makhana mix (small handful, about 30g)", "dinner_a": "Black-chana and vegetable stuffed paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Fish and vegetable cutlets (100g fish) with red rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few walnuts"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Your plate doesn''t need an audience", "notes": "You don''t need to demonstrate perfect eating, finish everything on your plate, or perform enthusiasm at every meal just because your baby is now at the table too. Your own hunger, fullness and preferences still matter, regardless of what''s happening on the rest of the table.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge (1 cup cooked) with milk (150ml) and a spoon of mixed seeds", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable uttapam (2 pieces, 2 eggs) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-vegetable jowar bowl (3/4 cup rajma, 1 cup jowar)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-vegetable dalia (100g mutton, 1 cup dalia)", "nourishment_break_a": "Cooked corn-chana chaat (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Moong dal khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with curd (150g) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a stir-fried cabbage", "still_hungry": "A banana with a small handful of almonds (about 15g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When cooking for two feels like too much", "notes": "Preparing a separate dish for your baby, on top of your own, can quietly eat into the time and energy you''d usually spend on your own meal. It''s completely fine to lean on whatever you can prepare quickly and well for yourself on a day like that — a reheatable dal, a simple curry, or the same few dishes on rotation. A fast, familiar meal for you is not a lesser one.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa (2 pieces, 2 eggs) with coconut chutney", "lunch_a": "Mixed-bean and barley stew (1 cup) with toast (2 slices)", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed bhindi (okra)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer (75g) and cucumber bowl with lemon", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry, dal (3/4 cup) and curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked), a cooked spinach sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with chopped papaya"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Feeding alongside, not always feeding your baby first", "notes": "It''s common to feed your baby''s entire meal before taking a single bite of your own — by the time you sit down, yours may be cold, or skipped altogether. A few small shifts can help protect your own meal: plate your own full meal before you start feeding your baby, ask another adult to take over for a few minutes so you can actually sit and eat, and lean on something that reheats well instead of a meal that goes cold every day. If eating is delayed, store your portion safely and reheat it when you are ready — refrigerate perishable food within two hours, or within one hour if the room is above 32°C. Aim to make enough food available for yourself, even if you eat it in stages — an interrupted meal still counts, and eating it a little later is a practical plan, not a failure. If your medication schedule depends on eating at set times, follow that timing rather than delaying the meal.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup cooked) with peanuts (1 tbsp) and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with multigrain toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice noodles (1 cup cooked) with tofu (75g) and cooked vegetables", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a spoon of peanut butter (1 tbsp) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with grated apple and a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked rice) with chana (1/2 cup) and raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Mutton and vegetable curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Your backup for a day that falls apart", "notes": "Some days nothing goes as planned — a feed runs long, a nap refuses to happen, or you simply run out of hands. On a day like that, a backup you can make on autopilot is worth more than an ambitious meal you don''t have the time or energy for: reheated dal, rice and curd; eggs and toast; or whatever you already keep on hand for exactly this.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup cooked) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Moong-corn handvo (2 pieces) with mint curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken and vegetable curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped apple with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted corn and paneer chaat (75g paneer)", "dinner_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish (100g) with millet-and-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (about 30g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When a shared meal isn''t actually easier", "notes": "Eating the same dish as everyone else at the table doesn''t always work out simpler — a spicier meal, a different schedule, or just wanting something else can make a separate plate the more realistic choice on a given day. That''s a reasonable call, not a failure to make things simpler.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with vegetables and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable appam (2 pieces, 2 eggs) with curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Multigrain roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry, dal (3/4 cup) and curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a stir-fried bell pepper", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet potato with curd (150g) and roasted cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with banana and a pinch of cinnamon", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal, 1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a sautéed vegetable and ghee", "dinner_b": "Mutton keema (100g) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Dinner B is swapped for a chicken option rather than repeating Day 1''s fish, so fish stays at three servings across the week rather than four. No forced choices today. Think back on one meal this week that felt easy to protect, and one that didn''t — what made the difference?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-lentil adai (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with vegetable appam (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Savoury bowl of millet (1 cup cooked), paneer (75g) and vegetables", "lunch_b": "Chicken-chana pulao (100g chicken, 1/2 cup chana) with cooked vegetables and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit with unsweetened yoghurt (150g) and a tablespoon of seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted soy-makhana mix (small handful, about 30g)", "dinner_a": "Black-chana and vegetable stuffed paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with red rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few walnuts"}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one thing that would make protecting your own meal feel a little easier day to day?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 44: When you can't think of what to cook anymore (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   44,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'When you can''t think of what to cook anymore',
   '"A repeated meal isn''t a lack of creativity — it''s one less decision on a day that already asked a lot of you."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the daily decision of what to eat — and making that decision smaller, not more elaborate. By nearly a year in, deciding what to eat — every single day, on top of everything else — can wear a person down more than the actual cooking does. This week isn''t about becoming a better cook or planning more elaborately. It''s about giving yourself permission to make that decision smaller: a short, repeatable rotation of meals you already know how to make, rather than a new idea every day.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: a repeatable meal rotation may make meal timing and carbohydrate amounts more predictable. The repeated meals still need to fit your established portions, medication timing and individual glucose-management plan. The A and B options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets rather than assuming either is interchangeable. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: a smaller, repeated rotation of meals can make it easier to stay consistently safe, once you have confirmed that each dish and all of its ingredients are safe for you. Recheck packaged ingredient labels occasionally, since formulations can change even for a food you buy regularly. If decision fatigue is turning into skipping meals rather than repeating them, or "I don''t know what to eat" is becoming "I''m not eating," that''s worth naming to someone — a partner, a friend, or your care team — rather than pushing through alone.',
   'A repeatable rotation works just as well for vegetarian and non-vegetarian meals — pick two or three dependable options within your usual protein sources and let them repeat without needing a new idea each time. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; that''s this week''s whole point. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a biryani, pulao or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today, choose the two or three meals in this week''s plan that already feel familiar. Those — not the most impressive options — are the beginning of your dependable rotation.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with a cucumber-tomato salad and rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped watermelon with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Vegetable biryani (1 cup cooked rice) with raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Pan-cooked fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few cashews (15g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A short list of ''good enough'' meals", "notes": "You don''t need a new idea every day. A short list of five or six meals you already know how to make, genuinely enjoy, and can put together without much thought is worth more than a much longer list you never actually use. Repeating a meal isn''t a sign you''ve run out of ideas — it''s one less decision on a day that already asked a lot of you.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Multigrain roti (2 pieces) with a chana masala (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a stir-fried bell pepper", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a spoon of almond butter (1 tbsp)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with grated cucumber and roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "A stuffed paratha (1 medium) using the sautéed cabbage sabzi saved from Day 1, with curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with millet-and-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Batch-cooking one thing, twice", "notes": "Cooking a double portion of one dal, curry or grain and reworking it into a second, slightly different meal a day or two later can cut your actual cooking decisions in half without anyone noticing it''s the same base. Some of today''s lunch and dinner options build on what you already cooked earlier this week. Worth knowing: Cool and refrigerate the portion you plan to reuse promptly — within two hours, or within one hour in very hot conditions. Use refrigerated leftovers within three to four days and reheat them until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar porridge (1 cup) with milk (200ml) and a spoon of chopped nuts (15g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "A wrap made with the grilled chicken (100g) saved from Day 1 and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped pear with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted chana and peanuts (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "A dal soup made from the toor dal saved from Day 1 (3/4 cup), loosened with water and lemon, with toast (2 slices)", "dinner_b": "Mutton keema wrap (100g mutton, roti wrap) with a cucumber-tomato salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with chopped papaya"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "The grocery list that thinks for you", "notes": "A repeating list of staple ingredients — the ones your go-to meals actually use — means grocery shopping stops being its own decision-making exercise too. Keep it written down somewhere you''ll actually see it again, and let it do the thinking the next time you''re too tired to plan from scratch.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable stuffed paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Pan-seared fish (100g fish) with lemon rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped mango with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and sautéed green beans", "still_hungry": "A banana with a small handful of walnuts (15g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When ''good enough'' still feels like giving up", "notes": "It''s common to feel a flicker of guilt over a repeated, simple meal — as though it says something about how much effort you''re putting in. A meal that''s nutritionally reasonable and actually gets eaten is doing its job, whether it took an hour or ten minutes to make.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and sautéed spinach", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped papaya with curd (150g) and seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer (75g) and cucumber cubes with chaat masala", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Baked fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pistachios (15g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Letting someone else decide sometimes", "notes": "You don''t have to be the one who decides every meal, every day. Asking a partner or family member to choose and make dinner occasionally, or ordering something in without a debate about whether you ''should,'' are both reasonable strategies — not signs you''ve stopped trying.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "A stuffed paratha (1 medium) using chana masala saved and frozen promptly from Day 2, thawed safely and reheated until steaming hot, with curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken biryani (1 cup cooked rice, 100g chicken) with raita (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped apple with a spoon of peanut butter (1 tbsp)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with pomegranate and a pinch of chaat masala", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal) (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meal from this week you''d happily put on repeat, without needing a reason.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich (2 eggs, 2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with a cucumber-tomato salad and rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped watermelon with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Vegetable biryani (1 cup cooked rice) with raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few cashews (15g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What could make that meal even easier to prepare, store or ask someone else to make?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 45: Feeding yourself well without spending more (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   45,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Feeding yourself well without spending more',
   '"Good nourishment does not have to depend on expensive foods — many of the most dependable foods are already everyday staples."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the everyday, often more affordable foods that already do a lot for you — and letting go of the idea that healthy has to mean expensive. A year of new expenses — for the baby, medical visits, everything that comes with this stage — often puts real pressure on the food budget too. Dal, eggs, curd, seasonal produce and locally available staples are often among the more economical sources of protein and nourishment, although prices and availability vary by location, season and household. None of them are a downgrade from something fancier — they''re simply doing the job well, often at a lower cost.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: lower-cost foods such as dal, seasonal vegetables, eggs and staple grains can fit into many diabetes meal plans, but portions, carbohydrate amounts and meal timing still need to follow your individual plan. Cutting back on meals because of cost can affect glucose management; tell your treating team if budget pressure is making regular meals difficult. The A and B options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets rather than assuming either is interchangeable. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: check that a cheaper substitute is actually safe for you before switching — a lower-cost alternative isn''t automatically allergen-free, and packaged budget brands can vary in their ingredients. If you''re regularly unable to afford enough food for yourself, that''s a real and common concern worth raising — with a partner, a trusted family member, or your care team — rather than carrying alone. It isn''t a personal failing.',
   'Depending on local prices and access, eggs, dal, curd and other familiar staples may provide economical protein options. Eggs and chicken can be included where they fit your household''s budget, while fish choices should depend on local cost, availability and lower-mercury guidance. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a curry or mixed dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A year of new expenses — for the baby, medical visits, everything that comes with this stage — often puts real pressure on the food budget too. This week isn''t about cutting corners on your own nutrition. It''s about noticing which foods already do double duty: genuinely affordable and genuinely worth eating.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a small handful of roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted chana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The everyday staples that already do the most", "notes": "Dal, eggs, curd, seasonal produce and locally available staples are often among the more economical sources of protein and nourishment, although prices and availability vary. None of them need to be treated as a downgrade from something fancier; they''re simply doing the job well, often at a lower cost.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a small seasonal fruit", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk and vegetable curry (1/2 cup soy chunks) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few peanuts (15g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Frozen and dried aren''t a downgrade", "notes": "Plain frozen vegetables can be a useful alternative when fresh produce is expensive, unavailable or likely to spoil before you use it. They remain nutritious choices; compare local prices and, where possible, choose versions without added sauces or seasoning. Dried beans and lentils, bought in bulk and soaked at home, are typically cheaper than pre-cooked or packaged versions of the same food, and tinned fish can be a shelf-stable protein option where a locally affordable, lower-mercury variety is available.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-stuffed paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with rajma curry (3/4 cup) made from dried, soaked rajma and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "lunch_b": "A locally affordable lower-mercury fish (100g) or egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of cooked sprouted moong chaat (1 cup, never raw) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a frozen mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a frozen-vegetable mix", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What ''superfood'' pricing doesn''t tell you", "notes": "A food isn''t more nutritious just because it''s imported, trending, or expensive. Many familiar, everyday foods already grown and eaten locally — dal, seasonal greens, curd, eggs — provide the same kinds of nutrients that a costlier ''superfood'' is often marketed for.", "breakfast_a": "Rava upma (1 cup) with vegetables and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala egg curry (2 eggs) with paratha (1 medium) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-dal curry (100g chicken, 1/2 cup dal) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with a spoon of roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of milk (200ml) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk and vegetable curry (1/2 cup soy chunks) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Reducing waste without extra effort", "notes": "Reducing waste can mean using clean, edible parts of vegetables that might otherwise be overlooked, freezing a safe portion before it spoils, and buying only what you realistically expect to cook. Wash produce thoroughly under running water, remove damaged areas, and discard any peel, stem, leaf or other part that is not normally considered edible.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable roll (2 eggs, roti wrap) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with dal (3/4 cup) containing finely chopped, thoroughly washed edible vegetable leaves or stems, with a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana (30g) with buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "A dal-and-vegetable soup (3/4 cup dal) made with clean edible vegetable trimmings, with toast (2 slices)", "dinner_b": "A locally affordable lower-mercury fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When the budget genuinely feels tight", "notes": "Some weeks and months, the honest answer is that money is tight, not just habits worth adjusting. That''s a real pressure, not a personal failing. If you haven''t already, it''s worth checking what government nutrition and maternal-health schemes you may be eligible for — several are covered in this app''s Wealth section — and worth talking to your care team if you''re genuinely going without.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge (1 cup) with milk (200ml) and chopped banana, with sweetness adjusted to preference", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with roti (2 pieces), a seasonal vegetable or fruit, and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Guava with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Mixed dal khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever food from this week gave you the most for the least — money, effort, or both.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a small handful of roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted chana (15g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What is one affordable staple you would like to keep stocked more consistently?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 46: When a fast is part of your faith (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   46,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'When a fast is part of your faith',
   '"Your faith and your baby''s needs don''t have to be in conflict — informed choice and a little preparation can help you protect both."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about religious and cultural fasting — a real part of many mothers'' lives — and practical, appropriately cautious information that supports your own informed choice, not a directive either way. Many mothers observe a religious or cultural fast at some point during this stage — Karva Chauth, Navratri, Ekadashi, Ramzan roza, Lent, or another tradition specific to your own faith and family. This week isn''t about telling you whether to fast. It offers practical, appropriately cautious information to support your decision. If you have a medical condition or health concern, discuss the proposed fast with your care team; if you want guidance about religious rules or alternatives, consult a trusted authority within your own tradition. The daily menus below remain general meal suggestions for you. On a non-fasting day, use them in the usual pattern. If your tradition restricts when or what you eat, use only the options that fit your practice, and combine them within the permitted eating window rather than treating every listed meal as something to fit into a fasting day. Restricting foods or moving meals into a fasting window doesn''t itself establish nutritional adequacy, and it doesn''t make a particular fast medically suitable for you — that judgment still belongs to your own care team.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: fasting can cause low or high glucose, dehydration and, for some people, serious complications. Do not independently skip, delay or change medication to fit a fast. Before fasting, obtain an individual plan covering medication timing, glucose monitoring and the level at which you must stop the fast. If glucose becomes too low or too high, or you develop symptoms of a hypo, marked hyperglycaemia or illness, break the fast and follow your established treatment plan. As with any week here, the A and B meal options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content — check which fits your plan before or after a fast. If you currently have anaemia, remain symptomatic, are taking treatment for iron deficiency or had significant blood loss and have not been told that you have recovered, a prolonged fast may make it harder to meet your food and fluid needs. Discuss whether the proposed fast is suitable for your present health.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. On a fasting day, use only the options that fit your own practice, and combine them within your permitted eating window rather than treating every listed meal as something to fit into that day. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Begin by identifying the kind of fast you are considering: whether it restricts timing, fluids, particular foods, or some combination of these. The practical plan depends on what the fast actually involves.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices), a seasonal vegetable or fruit, and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana (30g) with buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "What research says about fasting and your milk", "notes": "Research on fasting during breastfeeding is limited and mostly relates to Ramadan. Small studies have not consistently found major short-term changes in infant growth or the main components of breast milk, but the evidence is not strong enough to promise that fasting will affect every mother and baby in the same way. Individual responses can differ, particularly with long fasting hours, hot weather, illness, low intake or difficulty replacing fluids and food during the eating window.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with dates", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Not all fasts are the same", "notes": "Fasting traditions vary widely, and knowing which kind you''re considering matters. Some are complete, waterless fasts for a set period. Others allow water, milk, fruit or specific foods throughout the day. Others are time-restricted — no food or water during daylight hours, but a full meal before dawn and after sunset. Each can carry a different level of risk for a breastfeeding mother. Religious rules, exemptions and modifications differ between traditions, communities and individual circumstances. If you want religious guidance, consult a trusted authority within your own tradition.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken stew (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked) with raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Fasting while breastfeeding an older baby", "notes": "At this stage, your baby may be eating complementary foods while continuing to breastfeed. That can change the practical picture, but it does not make every fast automatically suitable. Your own health, milk supply, the type and length of the fast, the weather, your baby''s usual feeding pattern and any medical condition still matter. Continue following your baby''s established feeding and breastfeeding cues rather than changing the baby''s intake simply to make a fast possible.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge (1 cup) with milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a seasonal vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit with curd (150g) and seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled potato with curd (150g) and roasted peanuts (15g), if permitted within your practice", "dinner_a": "Sabudana khichdi (1 cup) with curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk curry (1/2 cup soy chunks) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you choose to fast", "notes": "If you do choose to fast, a few practical habits can help: choose nourishing food and fluids in whatever window your tradition allows, avoid pushing yourself through heavy chores or exertion that day, and pace yourself rather than eating a very large meal all at once. Continue responding to your baby''s usual breastfeeding and complementary-feeding cues. Do not force additional feeds or change the baby''s established feeding plan solely to compensate for the fast. Worth knowing: If your practice restricts which foods you eat rather than when you eat, build a complete plate from what''s allowed within your own tradition — for example combining a grain like sabudana or singhara atta with a protein source like curd, milk or nuts, and fruit — rather than relying mainly on sabudana, potato, fruit or sweet foods alone. If your tradition allows eating before and after a time-restricted fast: before the fast, choose a meal containing a staple grain or other carbohydrate, a protein food, vegetables or fruit, and fluids; after the fast, begin with fluids and a comfortable amount of food, then eat a balanced meal containing protein, carbohydrate and vegetables rather than relying mainly on fried or sugary foods; and if another eating opportunity is available, add curd, milk, fruit, dal, egg, roasted chana or another suitable protein-containing option.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup) with milk (200ml) and chopped fruit", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Signs to break the fast, or check in with your care team", "notes": "Stop fasting if you feel unwell — that''s a reasonable, common decision, not a failure. Confusion, collapse or difficulty staying awake are emergencies: seek emergency medical help immediately, and do not give food or drink to someone who is unconscious or cannot swallow safely. Also seek prompt medical advice, without waiting for a true emergency, if you cannot keep fluids down, pass very little urine, or develop concerning glucose readings. Worth knowing: Seek advice if your baby feeds markedly less than usual, has fewer wet diapers than usual, seems unusually sleepy or unwell, or if you have an ongoing concern about milk intake or growth. Choosing to stop or adapt a fast for your health, or your baby''s, is a reasonable and common decision.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup) with peanuts (15g) and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion uttapam (2 pieces) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a seasonal vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted chana (30g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal citrus fruit with curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. Whatever your own relationship with fasting looks like — whether you fast, modify it, or don''t — how did this week''s information change how you think about it, if at all?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices), a seasonal vegetable or fruit, and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A seasonal fruit with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana (30g) with buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'If you ever choose to fast, what support, preparation or modification would help you protect your health without feeling that you had failed your faith?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 47: Eating well when you're the one who's sick (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   47,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well when you''re the one who''s sick',
   '"You don''t have to eat perfectly while you''re unwell — just enough, often enough, to keep going."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. This week offers low-effort nourishment ideas for times when appetite and energy are reduced. It does not diagnose an illness or replace treatment for persistent fever, breathing difficulty, significant dehydration, mastitis or another condition requiring medical care. Being sick as a mother is different — there''s rarely a real day off, you may still be breastfeeding, and your appetite might be the last thing that feels manageable. This week is about practical, low-effort nourishment when you''re unwell, not a full recovery protocol. Little and often beats a perfect plate.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: illness — especially with vomiting, reduced eating, or fever — can genuinely affect your glucose in either direction. Don''t stop or change your medication on your own; ask your care team for a sick-day plan in advance, including how often to check your glucose and when to seek help. The A and B meal options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own plan, especially if appetite is reduced. If you have kidney, heart or another condition for which you have been given a fluid limit: follow that individual plan rather than general advice to drink more. If you suspect mastitis and are becoming significantly unwell, notice redness spreading rapidly, suspect an abscess, or see no improvement after about 12 to 24 hours of supportive care, contact your care team promptly.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety, and are easy to soften into softer, easier-to-manage forms when you''re not feeling well. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. When appetite is reduced, a smaller amount of either option, or the "if you''re still hungry" suggestion alone, is a completely reasonable meal — these are starting points, not requirements. On Day 5, some additions are marked "if tolerated": skip them without concern if they don''t sit well while you''re unwell. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece, and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today, identify two meals that require almost no decision-making and one person you could ask for practical help. A written backup plan is easier to use when illness has already reduced your energy.", "breakfast_a": "Suji chilla (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices), a seasonal fruit or cooked vegetable, and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "You can (usually) keep breastfeeding", "notes": "With many common illnesses — including a cold, influenza, COVID-19, diarrhoea and mastitis — breastfeeding can usually continue. However, it is too broad to say that infections never pass through breast milk. Different infections require different precautions. Respiratory illnesses such as influenza spread mainly through close contact and respiratory droplets rather than through breast milk. Worth knowing: Wash your hands before touching your baby or expressing milk, cover coughs and sneezes, clean pumping equipment carefully, and consider a mask when you have respiratory symptoms and close contact cannot be avoided. Tell the pharmacist or prescriber that you are breastfeeding before using a new medicine, including an over-the-counter cold remedy.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla (4 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken pulao (1 cup cooked rice, 100g chicken) with a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Warm milk (200ml) with toast or a banana", "dinner_a": "Dal-and-vegetable soup (3/4 cup dal) with toast (2 slices)", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Mastitis is different from a cold", "notes": "Mastitis is inflammation of the breast and may cause a painful, swollen, red or warm area, sometimes with fever or flu-like symptoms. Continue breastfeeding responsively if you can, including from the affected breast, but do not try to force the breast empty or express more milk than your baby needs. If feeding is too painful, hand-express only enough for comfort or to provide the milk your baby would ordinarily take. Rest, use a cold compress for comfort, avoid deep massage or firm pressure, and seek advice about pain relief that is appropriate for you. Worth knowing: Seek medical care sooner if you are becoming significantly unwell, redness is spreading rapidly, you suspect an abscess, or symptoms are worsening. If there is no improvement after approximately 12 to 24 hours of supportive care, contact a clinician.", "breakfast_a": "Dalia porridge (1 cup) with milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Curd rice (1 cup cooked rice mixed with curd, 150g) with a small portion of soft-cooked dal (1/4 cup) or a cooked vegetable, as tolerated", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Eating when you have no appetite", "notes": "Older advice about starving a fever or sticking to strict, bland foods has largely been replaced by simpler guidance: eat what you can tolerate, in small amounts, as often as you can manage. A few days of simple, repetitive meals — khichdi, toast, curd rice — is completely fine. This isn''t the week to worry about variety. A day or two of simpler food can be reasonable. As appetite returns, move back toward your usual balanced meals rather than remaining on a highly restricted \"bland\" diet without a medical reason.", "breakfast_a": "Soft moong-dal khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with curd (150g), if tolerated", "breakfast_b": "Toast (2 slices) with a boiled egg (1) and a glass of milk (200ml), if tolerated", "lunch_a": "Curd rice (1 cup cooked rice mixed with curd, 150g)", "lunch_b": "Shredded chicken (75g chicken) with soft rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g), if tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana", "nourishment_break_b": "Plain toast (2 slices)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-vegetable soup (75g paneer) with toast (2 slices)", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Replacing fluids when illness reduces your intake", "notes": "When fever, vomiting or diarrhoea causes fluid loss, take frequent small sips of fluids you can tolerate. Seek medical help if you cannot retain fluids or develop signs of significant dehydration. Worth knowing: If vomiting or diarrhoea is significant, an oral rehydration solution (ORS) can help replace fluid and electrolytes alongside food, not instead of it — prepare a packaged ORS product with exactly the amount of safe water stated on its label, and keep sipping it through the day as needed, separately from whatever food you can manage below.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal and rice congee (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g), if tolerated", "breakfast_b": "Dal-and-vegetable soup (3/4 cup dal) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml), if tolerated", "lunch_a": "Soft rice (1 cup cooked) with watery moong dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g), if tolerated", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-rice soup (75g chicken, 1 cup rice)", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk (200ml), if tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "A ripe banana, if tolerated", "dinner_a": "Dal-and-vegetable soup (3/4 cup dal) with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g), if tolerated", "dinner_b": "Egg-drop and vegetable soup (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml), if tolerated", "still_hungry": "A banana, curd or another simple food you tolerate"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to get medical help", "notes": "Seek urgent medical care for breathing difficulty, chest pain, confusion, fainting, blue or grey lips, severe dehydration, blood in vomit or stool, or if you appear seriously unwell. Seek prompt advice if vomiting prevents you from keeping fluids down, fever is persistent or worsening, or symptoms are not improving as expected. Worth knowing: If your baby seems unusually unwell, feeds far less than usual, or you''re worried about their breathing or hydration, seek prompt medical advice — check Child Care for more on recognising illness in your baby. It''s always reasonable to call for guidance rather than wait and wonder.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Nimbu paani with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g), if tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava or another seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk curry (1/2 cup soy chunks) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option. However this week finds you — well or recovering — what''s one simple meal you could lean on the next time you''re not feeling your best?", "breakfast_a": "Suji chilla (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices), a seasonal fruit or cooked vegetable, and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken keema (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Who could help protect one period of rest, prepare food, or take over part of the baby''s care when you are unwell?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 48: Eating well as breastfeeding changes (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   48,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well as breastfeeding changes',
   '"Whether breastfeeding continues, changes or ends, your nourishment can adjust without becoming a diet."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about how your own eating can adjust as breastfeeding changes — whether you''re continuing regular breastfeeding alongside complementary foods, reducing some feeds, combining breast milk and formula, or preparing to stop or having already stopped. At this stage, many babies are eating complementary foods while continuing to breastfeed. Continuing beyond the first birthday is valid, reducing feeds is valid, and stopping for personal or medical reasons is also valid. This week does not assume that any one of those paths is due now. Breastfeeding uses energy and nutrients, but the amount varies with how frequently your baby feeds, how much milk is transferred, your body and your wider circumstances. If feeds reduce, your appetite may change gradually — or it may not change noticeably at first. Neither response means you are doing anything wrong. This is not a cue to count calories, deliberately shrink portions or begin weight-loss dieting. Continue eating regular, satisfying meals and let hunger, fullness, energy and recovery guide gradual adjustments. This remains a nourishment plan for the mother, not a replacement-feeding plan for the baby. If breastfeeding is intentionally stopped before 12 months, replace breast milk with infant formula under the baby''s feeding guidance. Ordinary cow''s milk should not become the baby''s main drink before the first birthday.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: changes in breastfeeding, appetite and meal timing may affect glucose patterns differently from person to person. Continue your usual monitoring plan and ask for review if readings or intake change substantially. Do not alter medication or carbohydrate targets solely because a feed has been dropped. The A and B options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets rather than assuming either is interchangeable. If you were treated for iron-deficiency anaemia, or your periods are returning: see Day 4 below — your iron needs may look different now, and it''s worth checking with your care team rather than assuming the same plan still applies. However your feeding is going — continuing, changing, or ending by choice, for a medical reason, or because your milk supply didn''t work out the way you''d hoped — that''s valid, and it doesn''t require an explanation to anyone. Ending breastfeeding does not require an apology. Continuing breastfeeding does not require one either.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa, uttapam, chilla or frankie wrap is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao, biryani or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially given the iron considerations this week raises, and more so if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Notice where you are in your own feeding journey right now — not where you think you \"should\" be by this point. Let today''s plate follow your actual hunger, not an idea of how much you''re \"supposed\" to eat at this stage.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chole (chickpea) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-chana curry (100g chicken, 1/2 cup chana) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and a small handful of walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with chopped dates", "dinner_a": "Chana-and-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked rice, 1/2 cup chana) with raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Your appetite may change, or it may not", "notes": "Some mothers notice less hunger as breastfeeding reduces; others notice little immediate change, because sleep, activity, menstrual cycles, stress and individual biology also affect appetite. There is no required amount by which your food should decrease. Respond to your actual hunger rather than cutting food in anticipation.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton stew (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Cooked sprouted-moong salad (1 cup, never raw) with lemon and chopped tomato", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with a spoon of peanut butter (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers with a small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When some breastfeeds reduce and others remain", "notes": "Reducing one or more feeds does not create a precise new calorie target, and complementary feeding is not the same thing as ending breastfeeding. Keep your own meals regular and balanced while your appetite and routine find their new pattern.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a sabut moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken frankie (100g chicken, roti wrap) with a side salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana (15g) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Soy-chunk curry (1/2 cup soy chunks) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Iron and your changing needs", "notes": "Menstrual bleeding may resume before, during or after breastfeeding changes, and its timing varies widely. Once regular periods return, menstrual blood loss can influence iron needs, but the correct intake or supplement plan depends on your diet, bleeding pattern, symptoms, test results and previous iron status. Worth knowing: Persistent tiredness that doesn''t ease with rest, breathlessness on mild exertion, or pale skin or nail beds are worth mentioning to your care team, rather than assuming they''re just ordinary new-parent fatigue — they can check whether your iron needs any attention right now.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Spinach-and-egg toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-and-spinach curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a squeeze of lemon", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange or another vitamin-C-rich fruit with roasted chana (30g) — pairing vitamin C with iron-rich foods, as in today''s meals, helps your body absorb plant-based iron", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Palak dal (3/4 cup) with roti (2 pieces) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a small bowl of curd (150g) on the side", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If breastfeeding reduces quickly", "notes": "Sometimes breastfeeding reduces or stops faster than planned — for a medical reason, a return to work, or a difficult supply journey. Your eating doesn''t have to match that same fast timeline; let your appetite ease at its own pace over the following days and weeks. When possible, reducing feeds gradually over several weeks is usually more comfortable and lowers the chance of engorgement and mastitis. If feeds must stop more quickly, use a comfortable supportive bra, apply a cold compress for discomfort, and express only enough milk to relieve uncomfortable fullness — not repeatedly to empty the breasts. Worth knowing: If a baby under 12 months suddenly refuses the breast, don''t automatically assume that breastfeeding has naturally ended. Sudden refusal can be a temporary nursing strike, particularly during illness, discomfort or disruption — seek feeding guidance if it persists or intake is a concern. Also seek medical advice if you develop worsening breast pain, spreading redness, fever, feel significantly unwell, or suspect an abscess.", "breakfast_a": "Grilled vegetable sandwich (2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich (100g chicken, 2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice (1 cup cooked) with dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with a small bowl of roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with chopped dates and a few walnuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a green-moong-and-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Small, steady habits — settling in, not rushing", "notes": "There''s no need to force your plate back to exactly how it looked before pregnancy, or to reach some new \"normal\" by a certain date. Most mothers find their eating settles gradually into a steady, comfortable pattern over weeks, not days — let it happen at its own pace.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a mixed-dal (panchmel) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and ground roasted flaxseed", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd or milk (150g/200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds; tea or coffee may be served separately within your caffeine limit", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Wherever you are in your feeding journey — continuing, partway through a change, or already finished — what''s one part of how you''ve been eating these past weeks that you''d like to carry forward?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chole (chickpea) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-chana curry (100g chicken, 1/2 cup chana) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and a small handful of walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with chopped dates", "dinner_a": "Chana-and-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked rice, 1/2 cup chana) with raita (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'As your feeding routine changes, what would it look like to let your eating change just as gradually — without rushing to "fix" anything?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
