-- postpartum week 49: Eating well as you return to movement (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   49,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well as you return to movement',
   '"Building strength starts with fueling it, not restricting it."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about eating in a way that supports movement — whatever that looks like for you right now, from a short walk to a structured workout, or not yet begun at all. There is no single right time to return to exercise after birth, and this week does not assume you''re at any particular point. Gentle movement can often begin when it feels comfortable after an uncomplicated recovery. Structured, strenuous or high-impact exercise may need a more gradual return, particularly if you had medical or surgical complications or still have pain, leaking, pressure, heaviness, persistent abdominal doming or another ongoing symptom. Movement asks something of your body, the same way healing and feeding do — it deserves real fuel, not less food. This isn''t a week about earning your meals through exercise, or about using movement as a shortcut to losing weight quickly. Eating enough, staying hydrated, and resting between sessions all matter as much as the movement itself.',
   'If you had a caesarean birth or significant tearing, or have persistent diastasis recti: the way you return to exercise depends on how you have healed and whether symptoms remain. Seek individual guidance before strenuous or high-impact exercise if you still have pain, wound concerns, pelvic pressure or heaviness, leaking, or persistent abdominal doming. Diastasis does not mean avoiding all core movement; a pelvic health physiotherapist can help you find exercises appropriate for your body. If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: activity can affect glucose patterns, and the response may change with the type, duration and intensity of movement. Follow your usual monitoring plan. If you use insulin or another medicine that can cause low glucose — or your diabetes team has advised it — carry a fast-acting carbohydrate source and know how to treat a low. Ask your diabetes team before changing medication, carbohydrate targets or timing around activity. The A and B options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own plan before adjusting portions around activity. However much or little movement is part of your life right now, that''s the right amount for you — this week supports whichever path you''re on, not a particular pace.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa, uttapam, chilla or frankie wrap is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao, biryani or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Notice where you actually are with movement right now — a short walk, structured exercise, or not yet started — rather than where you think you should be. Let today''s plate match what your body is actually doing today, not a future workout plan.", "breakfast_a": "Sprouted-moong-and-vegetable cheela (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma-and-vegetable curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "There''s no fixed timeline", "notes": "There is no single timeline for returning to movement after birth. Some mothers feel comfortable beginning gentle activity early; others need longer, especially after a complicated birth or difficult recovery. Build gradually from where your body is now. Seek individual guidance before strenuous or high-impact exercise if you have ongoing pain, leaking, pelvic pressure or heaviness, persistent abdominal doming, wound concerns or another symptom.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya (broken-wheat) upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted makhana (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Banana with a spoon of almond butter (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup), dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fueling before and after you move", "notes": "For a short walk or gentle movement, you may not need any specially timed food — your regular meals and your own hunger may be enough. Before a longer or more demanding session, a familiar carbohydrate-containing snack can support energy. Afterwards, an ordinary meal or snack containing both carbohydrate and protein can support recovery. None of this needs to be complicated or precisely timed.", "breakfast_a": "Stuffed methi paratha (1 medium) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked rice, 100g chicken)", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small handful of dates (20g) and a glass of milk (200ml), if you want something before a longer or more demanding session", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted chana (30g), after moving or whenever it fits your hunger", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Exercise and breastfeeding", "notes": "Regular moderate exercise does not reduce milk production or adversely affect milk composition or infant growth. You do not need to delay breastfeeding after moving — feed your baby whenever they want. Worth knowing: Feeding or expressing before exercise may make your breasts feel more comfortable. Drink according to thirst. If your baby seems bothered by the salty taste of sweat after a particularly sweaty session, rinsing your breast or showering may help, but this is not usually necessary.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-lobia curry (100g chicken, 1/2 cup lobia) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with a small bowl of roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Palak dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If your recovery has been slower", "notes": "A caesarean birth, significant tearing, or ongoing pelvic floor or core symptoms can mean movement needs more time and individual guidance — that''s a normal, common path, not a setback. Eating enough overall, including regular protein-rich foods, supports tissue healing regardless of when structured exercise itself resumes. Worth knowing: If you notice a visible bulge or doming along your abdominal midline, ongoing heaviness or bulging in your pelvic area, leaking urine, or pain that isn''t settling, mention it to your care team or ask about a pelvic floor physiotherapist before resuming structured exercise.", "breakfast_a": "Oats-and-vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Grilled chicken tikka roll (100g chicken) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked) with raita (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chole curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana (30g) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal fruit with curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Strength is built on fuel, not on eating less", "notes": "Using movement to justify eating less works against you — your body needs steady nourishment to build strength and stamina. For ordinary or moderate movement, drink according to thirst and continue your regular meals. Longer sessions, very heavy sweating or hot conditions may require additional fluid or electrolytes, but they are not automatically necessary, and this still is not a week to count calories.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Tandoori-style chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with roasted sunflower seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Curd rice (1 cup cooked rice mixed with curd, 150g) with dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Palak paneer (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (30g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. However movement fits into your life right now — a lot, a little, or not yet — what''s one thing that would make it easier to keep feeding yourself well alongside it?", "breakfast_a": "Sprouted-moong-and-vegetable cheela (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma-and-vegetable curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat movement as something your body deserves fuel for, rather than something you have to earn your food through?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 50: Eating well if you're thinking about another pregnancy (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   50,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well if you''re thinking about another pregnancy',
   '"Wherever you are on this decision, eating well doesn''t have to wait for an answer."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient of its own. It''s about a decision some mothers are actively weighing around now — whether and when another pregnancy might come next — and how eating well fits around that thinking, whichever way it goes. There''s no single right interval between pregnancies. Guidance from bodies like ACOG generally advises against conceiving again within six months of giving birth, and suggests discussing the risks and benefits of an interval under about 18 months with your care team — but this is a personal decision shaped by your health, your family, and your own readiness, not a rule to follow on its own. What eating well can do, regardless of timing, is support your health now and help address anything that may matter before a future pregnancy. That can include restoring iron status after pregnancy, birth blood loss or resumed periods, and maintaining adequate folate intake. Neither iron nor folic-acid supplements should be treated as a substitute for individual medical guidance. It''s also worth knowing that ovulation can return before your first postpartum period does. By this point, breastfeeding on its own isn''t a reliable way to prevent pregnancy — worth keeping in mind whether or not another pregnancy is something you want right now. If you do not want another pregnancy yet, ask your healthcare professional about a contraceptive option that suits you. If you''re not thinking about another pregnancy now — or ever — the meals still apply to you. Iron-rich and folate-rich foods support ordinary health, but this week does not require you to make a reproductive plan.',
   'If you had a caesarean birth or a complicated delivery: the interval before another pregnancy is worth discussing specifically with your care team, partly because of how your uterine scar has healed — this is individual, and not something a calendar alone can answer. If you''re anaemic or your iron levels haven''t fully recovered: ask about rechecking your iron levels before trying to conceive again, rather than assuming time alone has corrected it — correcting a deficiency beforehand is generally easier than catching up during a new pregnancy. If you have diabetes or prediabetes: the A and B meal options each day aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content — check which specific dishes fit your own targets, and adjust the rice, roti or grain portion to match your usual plan rather than assuming either option is interchangeable. If pregnancy may be part of your plans, ask for a preconception review of your glucose pattern, medicines and supplements. Do not change medication or severely restrict carbohydrates on your own. If you have PCOS: pairing a protein or fibre source with carbohydrate-containing meals, as most of this week''s dishes already do, and keeping meals regular rather than skipped can support insulin sensitivity — this is a general pattern, not a prescribed diet, and any specific plan should still come from your own care team. Irregular periods do not mean pregnancy cannot occur, so use contraception even with irregular cycles if avoiding pregnancy matters to you right now. If you have an underactive thyroid treated with levothyroxine: thyroid hormone needs typically rise in pregnancy, and levels are usually rechecked and adjusted before or very early in a pregnancy. Ask your care team to review your dose and levels if another pregnancy is part of your plan. Levothyroxine is usually taken on an empty stomach, and calcium- or iron-containing foods — including the curd or milk in many of this week''s breakfasts — can reduce its absorption if eaten too close to your dose; follow your prescribed timing, and feel free to move a meal''s dairy component to a later eating occasion if that fits your medication instructions better. A different thyroid condition or treatment may follow a different pattern — check with your own care team. However you''re thinking about timing — soon, later, or not at all — that''s your decision to make, not something this week is nudging you toward.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; a dhokla or cheela is 2-4 pieces and toast is 2 slices. In a khichdi, pulao or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, folate, vitamin B12 and vitamin D are worth particular attention this week especially, and more so if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Some mothers reading this are actively weighing another pregnancy; others aren''t thinking about it at all, and both are completely fine. Let today''s plate be about feeding yourself well right now, not about a decision that may or may not be on your mind.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g), peanuts (15g) and a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a matki (moth bean) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken do-pyaza (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish tikka (100g fish) with a sautéed vegetable and roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If this isn''t on your mind at all", "notes": "If another pregnancy isn''t something you''re thinking about — now, later, or ever — this week doesn''t need you to. There''s nothing here that requires a plan or a decision; it''s simply what eating well can look like at this point, whichever way things go.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dhokla (4 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and lauki (bottle gourd) sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Apple slices with a spoon of almond butter (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup), a cooked vegetable and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer tikka (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Replenishing iron, again", "notes": "Iron stores can run low after pregnancy and birth — especially after heavier blood loss during delivery, if you were anaemic beforehand, or once your periods return and start drawing on iron again each month. This is about pregnancy, birth, and your own cycle, not about how you''re feeding your baby. Pairing iron-rich foods with something vitamin-C-rich at the same meal helps your body absorb more of it, so today''s plate leans on that pairing. Worth knowing: If you''re trying to conceive or could already be pregnant, avoid liver and avoid additional supplements containing retinol or high-dose preformed vitamin A. Do not combine multiple prenatal or multivitamin products unless your care team has checked them. Beta-carotene in ordinary foods is not the concern. If you had heavy bleeding, persistent fatigue, breathlessness or previously low iron, ask whether your blood count and iron status should be checked.", "breakfast_a": "Besan-and-spinach cheela (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup), a squeeze of lemon, and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-onion salad with lemon", "nourishment_break_a": "Orange segments with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh amla, guava or orange with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a spinach-and-moong dal (3/4 cup), a squeeze of lemon, and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato-cucumber salad with lemon", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Folate, again", "notes": "Folate needs rise sharply in early pregnancy, often before most people even know they''ve conceived — which is why the standard advice is to start 400 micrograms of folic acid daily before you begin trying, not after a positive test. If another pregnancy is anywhere in your thinking, this is worth raising with your care team now rather than later. Worth knowing: The standard dose is 400 micrograms daily, continued through the first 12 weeks of pregnancy. A higher dose is sometimes advised — for example, after a previous pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect, with certain medications, or with diabetes — but that decision should be made with your care team, not increased on your own.", "breakfast_a": "Green-moong-and-methi cheela (2 pieces) with curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-methi bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-and-spinach curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Guava with a small handful of roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Cooked sprouted-moong salad (1 cup, never raw) with lemon and cucumber", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-methi curry (3/4 cup), a sautéed vegetable and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-methi bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you''re breastfeeding during a new pregnancy", "notes": "Breastfeeding can generally continue during an uncomplicated pregnancy if it remains comfortable for you and your child. Continue regular, satisfying meals, drink according to thirst and rest when possible. Pregnancy hormones can reduce milk supply, and this is not something a mother can always prevent by eating more. Worth knowing: At Week 50, your current baby is still under 12 months. If you notice signs that milk supply may be dropping — fewer wet nappies, persistent dissatisfaction after feeds, or a concern about growth — seek infant-feeding guidance rather than judging supply from breast fullness alone; check Child Care for more if supply needs supplementing. If the pregnancy involves bleeding, significant pain, risk of preterm birth or another complication, ask the pregnancy care team whether continuing breastfeeding is appropriate.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain toast (2 slices) with peanut butter (1 tbsp), banana slices and a glass of milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a whole-moong curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton rogan-josh-style curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small banana-and-date milkshake (200ml milk, no added sugar) — or roasted nuts with milk instead if you''re watching your glucose", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana chaat (30g) with cucumber, tomato and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-and-spinach dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with a cooked vegetable and roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "This isn''t a countdown", "notes": "Whatever you decide — trying again soon, waiting years, or not planning another pregnancy at all — that decision is yours, on your own timeline, and this week isn''t trying to nudge you toward any of it. Eating well doesn''t need to wait for a decision either way.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi (1 cup) with roasted peanuts (15g) and curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with a vegetable sandwich (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Tandoori-style paneer (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken bhuna (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Whatever you''re thinking about another pregnancy — soon, later, not at all, or genuinely undecided — what''s one thing you''d want your body to have more of before you''d feel ready, whether or not that day ever comes?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g), peanuts (15g) and a squeeze of lemon", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a matki (moth bean) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken do-pyaza (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with a small bowl of curd (150g) and roasted walnuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish tikka (100g fish) with a sautéed vegetable and roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let your eating support your own readiness, on your own timeline, rather than a calendar built for someone else?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 51: Eating well as your baby's first birthday approaches (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   51,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well as your baby''s first birthday approaches',
   '"You''ve spent a year feeding everyone else — today''s a good day to remember your own plate too."',
   'Many families begin preparing for a baby''s first birthday around now — or may celebrate a little early — with sweets, guests, photographs and sometimes a ritual or puja. It is easy for the mother in the middle of everything to barely eat a proper meal herself. This week isn''t about restricting the celebration food. It''s about making sure a plate of your own is part of the day too, not an afterthought squeezed in once everyone else has been served. If you have diabetes, PCOS, or are otherwise watching your blood sugar, a day with concentrated sweets is worth a small amount of planning, not avoiding the celebration altogether. Whatever this milestone looks like for your family — a big celebration, a quiet day, or something in between — this week is simply about making sure you''re fed too. This remains a nourishment plan for the mother, not a birthday menu for the baby. If your baby is still under 12 months, check Child Care for birthday-food safety guidance — honey, textures, and what adult cake or mithai does and doesn''t suit a baby that age.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: follow your usual meal, monitoring and medication plan on a celebration day. Eating a sweet with or after a regular meal may be easier to manage than replacing a meal with sweets, but protein or fibre does not cancel the carbohydrate. Do not skip meals, medication or planned doses to compensate. If you have PCOS or prediabetes but do not use glucose-lowering medicine: you do not need to avoid the celebration. Continue regular meals and follow any individual glucose guidance you have already been given. If you''re breastfeeding: a busy hosting day can mean skipped meals and low fluid intake without you quite noticing, and that tends to affect your energy and comfort faster than on an ordinary day — keeping water within reach and eating at roughly your usual times helps. However your family marks this milestone — or doesn''t — that''s entirely yours to decide; this week is about your own plate, not a particular kind of celebration.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa, uttapam, chilla or adai is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a pulao, biryani or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "A year of feeding a baby often means a year of half-eaten meals, cold tea, and \"I''ll eat after.\" Today, notice whether that''s still true for you — and if it is, let today be the day you eat before you''re starving, not after.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal-and-vegetable adai (2 pieces) with chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken chettinad (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted pistachios (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''re the one hosting", "notes": "If you''re the one organizing today — the guests, the sweets, the photos — it''s easy to end up eating standing up, in between everything else, or not at all until it''s over. Setting aside your own plate before the day gets busy is worth the two minutes it takes.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo paratha (1 medium) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast or roti (2 pieces), a fruit bowl and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a mixed-vegetable sambar (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana (15g) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Seasonal fruit with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Sweets don''t need to be earned or feared", "notes": "There may be cake, mithai or both, and that is completely fine. Skipping meals to \"make room\" can leave you excessively hungry and uncomfortable later. Let the sweet sit alongside or after a regular meal rather than replacing the meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with paratha (1 medium) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rajma-vegetable pulao (1 cup cooked rice, 1/2 cup rajma) with raita (150g)", "lunch_b": "Chicken biryani (1 cup cooked rice, 100g chicken) with raita (150g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A portion of cake or mithai you enjoy, with or after a regular meal; follow your individual carbohydrate guidance if applicable — roasted nuts (15g) may be added if wanted", "nourishment_break_b": "Cucumber-and-mint raita (150g)", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Chicken korma (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re managing your blood sugar today", "notes": "A celebration does not require you to avoid every sweet. Keep your usual meals as steady as the day allows and fit celebration food around the diabetes or glucose plan that normally works for you. Pairing carbohydrate with protein or fibre may help some people, but it does not make the carbohydrate disappear. Worth knowing: If you use insulin or another medicine that can cause low glucose, follow your established monitoring and medication plan. Do not skip a planned meal or dose to compensate for sweets. If celebration timing will substantially disrupt your usual routine, ask your diabetes team beforehand how to handle it. As with any week here, the A and B meal options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which fits your own plan.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken stew (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana (30g) with a small apple and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer cubes (75g paneer) with cucumber sticks", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Rajma curry (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If today feels bigger than just a birthday", "notes": "A first birthday can bring up more than cake and photographs — pride, exhaustion, maybe a few unexpected tears, or a strange mix of all three as you look back on the year. However today feels, there''s no right way to feel it. Eating something yourself, even a small plate, helps you actually be present for whichever feelings show up.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-potato curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a green-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Palak paneer (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Tomorrow, it''s back to ordinary", "notes": "One day of celebration doesn''t undo a year of steady eating, and it doesn''t need a strict day-after either. If yesterday included more sugar or richer food than usual, the plan for today is simply an ordinary day — regular meals, plenty of water, nothing to prove.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a sambar (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A boiled egg with a seasonal fruit and a glass of milk (200ml)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with an urad dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Matar paneer (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Looking back on this past year of feeding a baby — and yourself, some days more than others — what''s one thing you''d want to carry forward, and one thing you''d want to let go of?", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal-and-vegetable adai (2 pieces) with chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken chettinad (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted pistachios (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let today''s celebration include you, fully — not just as the one who fed everyone else?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 52: Completing your first year — and continuing to nourish yourself (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   52,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Completing your first year — and continuing to nourish yourself',
   '"A year of feeding a baby has also been a year of feeding yourself — that doesn''t end today."',
   'Fifty-two weeks ago, this series started with a simple idea: that the mother feeding a new baby needed feeding too. That idea doesn''t expire today. Whatever your situation is right now — still breastfeeding, weaned months ago, feeding formula, somewhere in between — is exactly right for you. There''s no single point everyone is supposed to have reached by a baby''s first birthday. If you''re still breastfeeding, that is entirely normal. WHO recommends continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for up to two years or beyond. Continuing is valid, reducing feeds is valid, and stopping is valid; this week does not rank those paths. If you''ve stopped breastfeeding, some nutrition needs may change, but there is no single across-the-board reset. Your appetite, menstrual cycle, diet, previous deficiencies, medical conditions and future-pregnancy plans all matter. Regular, varied meals remain useful regardless of the feeding stage. Recovery does not run on a strict calendar. Sleep, energy and pelvic or core symptoms can continue changing after the first year. But persistent or worsening fatigue, pain, heavy bleeding, low mood or marked hair shedding deserves attention rather than being dismissed as "just postpartum."',
   'If you''re breastfeeding beyond 12 months: WHO supports continued breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for up to two years or beyond. Continue regular, satisfying meals and drink according to thirst. The amount you need may still vary with feeding frequency, appetite, activity and individual health. If you''ve stopped breastfeeding or never breastfed: do not automatically stop, continue or reduce supplements simply because the calendar has reached one year. Iron needs can change when periods return, while calcium recommendations do not necessarily change. Review supplements according to your diet, symptoms, test results and care-team guidance. If you have anaemia, heavy periods or persistent fatigue: ask whether your blood count and iron status should be checked rather than assuming the first year has corrected everything. If you have thyroid disease, diabetes, prediabetes or PCOS: continue the monitoring and medication plan already made with your care team. Reaching one year postpartum is not a reason to change medication, supplements or carbohydrate targets independently. The A and B options are not carbohydrate-matched. If you have prescribed carbohydrate targets, use those when choosing portions. PCOS alone does not mean you must count carbohydrates or follow a restrictive diet. However you''re feeding your baby, and however you''re feeding yourself, right now — that''s the right starting point for whatever comes next, not a report card on the year behind you.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa, cheela, thepla or bhurji-style dish is judged by the quantities stated, and toast is 2 slices. In a khichdi, pulao or other mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Fifty-two weeks of this series come down to one plain idea: however you''re eating today is a fine place to be. Nothing about reaching this week requires you to have arrived anywhere in particular.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sooji (rava) upma (1 cup) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted cashews (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish moilee (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''re still breastfeeding", "notes": "Many mothers continue breastfeeding beyond the first birthday alongside complementary foods. If that remains comfortable for you and your child, your meals can continue in the same flexible pattern: regular, satisfying food, enough fluid and adjustments based on your actual hunger and feeding routine.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a urad-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton stew (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted makhana (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates with a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a black urad dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you''ve stopped breastfeeding", "notes": "If breastfeeding has ended — recently or months ago — there is no automatic need to reduce portions or begin dieting. Appetite may change gradually, or not noticeably at first. Continue regular meals and adjust according to hunger, energy, activity, menstrual patterns and individual health.", "breakfast_a": "Methi-and-besan thepla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mixed-vegetable curry (3/4 cup) and dal (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-vegetable curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts (15g) with an apple and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a green-gram dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Recovery beyond the first year", "notes": "Reaching one year does not create a deadline for strength, sleep, mood or pelvic recovery. Some changes continue gradually. What matters is distinguishing ordinary variation from symptoms that remain persistent, worsen or interfere with daily life. Worth knowing: Seek healthcare support for persistent pain, heavy or unusual bleeding, pelvic pressure, leaking, low mood, breathlessness, marked fatigue or anything else that worries you. Most postpartum hair shedding improves by the first birthday; if substantial shedding continues or normal fullness has not returned, ask whether another cause should be assessed.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Egg-and-potato curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana (30g) with a glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a tinda-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The habits that outlast this year", "notes": "Eating enough, eating a variety, drinking water, and not treating food as something to earn or apologize for — none of that was ever really about \"postpartum.\" Those are just how eating well works, and they''re worth keeping long after any label attached to this year falls away.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with multigrain toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts (15g) with a banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of soaked chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a whole-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Wherever you go from here", "notes": "This first year does not have to end with a graduation test. Keep whichever parts of these plans genuinely helped — regular meals, flexible alternatives, nourishment breaks or simply remembering to feed yourself. As Nourish moves into its next stage, the chart remains a guide, not a report card.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-dal khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with vegetable poha (1 cup) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a mixed-dal curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted makhana (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-peas curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Looking back across this first year, what''s one way of eating you would like to carry into the next stage — and one expectation you are ready to leave behind?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sooji (rava) upma (1 cup) with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted cashews (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of ground roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish moilee (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few dates"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to keep feeding yourself well simply because you deserve it — not because you need to complete a stage perfectly?',
   'This week completes Nourish''s first postpartum year, not the whole journey. Next week, Nourish continues into the next stage with the same central promise: the plan can evolve as your life does, and you still deserve to be fed well.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 53: Eating well when the pressure to "get your body back" begins (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   53,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when the pressure to "get your body back" begins',
   '"Your body carried and fed a whole person this year — it was never lost, and it doesn''t need to be won back."',
   'Somewhere around a baby''s first birthday, a particular kind of pressure tends to sharpen: comments about "getting your body back," ads timed to a season or a sale, or a sense that recovery should be visibly "done" by now. None of that is a signal about your health — it''s timing, not evidence. There is no single medically required appearance — or universal timetable — for a mother''s body after birth. Weight, shape, appetite and recovery vary considerably between women. Severe restriction or rapid weight-loss diets can leave a mother under-fuelled and may be particularly difficult while breastfeeding. Regular, adequate meals are a safer foundation. Breastfeeding energy needs vary according to feeding frequency, activity, health and individual circumstances, so appetite and personalised medical advice matter more than a fixed calorie rule. Persistent distress about body shape or weight can affect emotional wellbeing and has been associated with depressive symptoms during pregnancy and the postpartum period. If these thoughts are becoming frequent, overwhelming or are changing how you eat, move or relate to yourself, they deserve compassionate support. If you notice yourself skipping meals, hiding what or how much you eat, feeling significant guilt after eating, or exercising to "earn" food, that''s worth raising with a trusted professional. This week''s meals aren''t a substitute for that kind of individualized support.',
   'If you have a history of disordered eating, or you''re noticing new patterns like skipping meals, secretive eating or guilt around food: treat that as a cue to reach out to a trusted healthcare provider or therapist rather than pushing through it alone — this series can''t replace that kind of individualized care. If you''re still breastfeeding: your energy needs may remain higher than before pregnancy, depending partly on how frequently your child feeds and how much milk is being produced. Avoid severe restriction and let hunger, energy and individual guidance shape your intake. If you have PCOS, diabetes, prediabetes or thyroid disease: keep following the monitoring and treatment plan you already have with your care team; a body-image milestone like a birthday isn''t a medical reason to change medication or targets on your own. The A and B options are not carbohydrate-matched. If you have prescribed carbohydrate targets, use those when choosing portions. PCOS alone does not mean you must count carbohydrates or follow a restrictive diet. However your body has changed this year, and however you feel about it today — you don''t owe anyone a "before and after," and this week''s meals are about steady nourishment, not a race back to anywhere. You may choose a weight-related goal or no weight goal; either way, nourishment and freedom from pressure remain important.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa/uttapam/chilla is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Whatever your body looks like right now isn''t a problem waiting for a solution. It did an enormous amount of work this year, and today''s meals are simply about feeding the person you actually are — not the one an ad or a comment thinks you should be chasing.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat, 1 cup) with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Fully cooked sprouted-moong chaat (1 cup, never raw) with lemon and a glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia (black-eyed pea) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If you''re still breastfeeding", "notes": "Nursing your child past their first birthday doesn''t change what regular meals are for: keeping you fuelled, not building or maintaining any particular shape. Eat according to hunger, not according to a calendar or a comment someone made.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with jowar roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a matki (moth bean) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Prawn curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates with a glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of fully cooked sprouts (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you''ve stopped breastfeeding", "notes": "If you''re not breastfeeding — whether that ended recently or a while back — use hunger alongside your energy, activity, health needs and any existing care plan, not a chart of how much less you should now be eating. Regular meals matter exactly as much as they did in week one.", "breakfast_a": "Moong-besan chilla (2 pieces) with coriander chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a vegetable kadhi (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chickpeas (30g) with an orange and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few roasted makhana (15g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "When \"get your body back\" talk comes from someone close to you", "notes": "A relative''s comment, a friend''s compliment about \"finally losing the weight,\" or a stranger''s remark can land hard, even when well meant. You''re allowed to redirect the conversation, say nothing, or simply let it pass without treating it as instruction for what to eat today. Worth knowing: If comments about your body are frequent enough to affect your mood, your eating, or how you feel about yourself, it''s worth naming that to someone you trust — a partner, a friend, or a professional. You don''t have to manage that weight alone.", "breakfast_a": "Poha (1 cup) with peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chole curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted watermelon seeds (15g) with a banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava with roasted chana (30g) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a green moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Movement without a body project attached", "notes": "If you''re moving more these days — walking, a class, chasing a toddler — let food follow that energy rather than trying to offset it. Movement here is about capability and enjoyment, not a transaction to earn dinner.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a horse gram (kollu) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-drumstick curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Guava with roasted cashews (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana masala (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken masala (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If old habits start creeping back in", "notes": "For some mothers, this looser stage can also loosen old, less helpful patterns — skipping meals to \"make up for\" something, weighing yourself repeatedly, or feeling like today''s food has to correct yesterday''s. None of that has to become routine again; noticing it early is enough reason to ask for support. Worth knowing: A pattern that persists — restriction, bingeing, purging, compulsive exercise, or intense guilt around food — deserves proper support rather than willpower. Speak with your doctor or a mental-health professional; this is common enough to ask about directly, and help works.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney, sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable-and-egg paratha (1 medium, 1 egg) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a mixed-bean curry (1/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Egg-and-potato masala (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana (15g) with an apple and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted jeera powder", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-methi dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-capsicum curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A few dates with a glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. What''s one thing you''d want to say to a mother who feels she should already have her \"old body back\" by now — and can you offer yourself that same sentence?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat, 1 cup) with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lauki-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Fully cooked sprouted-moong chaat (1 cup, never raw) with lemon and a glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia (black-eyed pea) curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it feel like to let this body simply be the one you''re living in right now — worth feeding well, without needing to look any particular way to deserve it?',
   'Next week continues Nourish''s next stage, still built around the same idea: your plate matters on its own terms, not as a reward or a correction for how you look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 54: Eating well as your period returns (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   54,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as your period returns',
   '"A returning cycle is another change to notice — not a recovery score, and not a cue to restrict what your body is asking for."',
   'Many mothers notice their period returning sometime in this first or second year, sometimes as early as a few months after birth and sometimes well beyond a year, particularly if still breastfeeding. There''s no single correct timeline, and where you fall on it isn''t something diet controls. Once periods resume, menstrual blood loss can add to ongoing iron needs. Iron remained important while periods were absent, particularly after pregnancy and birth; its regular inclusion may become even more relevant when bleeding returns. Some women notice greater hunger or stronger food cravings before a period. Research has found higher average energy intake during the luteal phase, although experiences vary considerably between people and cycles. Strict "cycle-syncing" meal plans are popular, but tightly structured phase-by-phase eating isn''t well supported by current evidence. Steady, adequate meals with enough protein, fat, fibre and key minerals cover most cycle-related needs more reliably than a rigid schedule. It''s also worth remembering that ovulation can happen before a period visibly returns, so the absence of a period isn''t a guarantee about fertility either way — relevant if you''re avoiding or hoping for another pregnancy right now.',
   'If your period hasn''t returned yet: continued breastfeeding can delay its return, and timing varies considerably. But if you are no longer breastfeeding — or the absence is unexpected for your feeding pattern, contraception or health history — mention it to your healthcare provider. No particular food is proven to restart a period, and pregnancy should be considered because ovulation can occur before the first visible period. If your periods have returned heavier, more painful or more irregular than before pregnancy: mention this to your care team rather than assuming it will settle on its own — it''s a reasonable thing to have checked rather than a normal condition of new motherhood. If you have anaemia, a history of heavy periods, or new fatigue since your cycle returned: continue any iron monitoring or supplement plan already agreed with your care team, and mention the change so it can be reviewed rather than adjusting it yourself. Wherever your cycle is right now — absent, irregular, or back to its old rhythm — that''s simply where your body is, not a score on how well you''ve recovered.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially given the iron considerations this week raises, and more so if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere in this first or second year, many mothers notice their period returning — sometimes earlier, sometimes much later, sometimes with little warning at all. There''s no fixed schedule it''s supposed to follow, and today''s meals don''t need to change because of where you are in that process.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted cumin and roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "If your period hasn''t returned yet", "notes": "Whether you''re breastfeeding, back at work, or both, it''s common for your period to take months longer to return than a friend''s did, or not to have returned at all yet. Continued breastfeeding can explain a long delay, but if you''ve stopped breastfeeding, aren''t pregnant or on period-suppressing contraception, and your period still hasn''t returned, that''s worth mentioning to your care team rather than assuming it will sort itself out.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a channa dal (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-potato curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Coconut water with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few roasted peanuts (15g) with a glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If your period has returned", "notes": "Once periods resume, menstrual blood loss can add to ongoing iron needs. Iron remained important while periods were absent, particularly after pregnancy and birth; its regular inclusion may become even more relevant now that bleeding has returned. Pairing iron-rich lentils, beans and greens with vitamin-C-rich fruit can help your body absorb more of the non-haem iron they carry. Diagnosed anaemia still needs proper assessment and prescribed supplementation from your care team — food alone isn''t a substitute for that.", "breakfast_a": "Spinach-and-egg toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with an orange or guava and a glass of milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Ragi porridge (1 cup) with nuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a lemon-rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a leafy green vegetable", "lunch_b": "Keema (minced mutton, 100g) curry with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Dates with ground roasted sesame seeds and an orange, plus a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Guava or orange with roasted chana (30g) and a glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lemon-chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a leafy green vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with dates"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "In the days before your period", "notes": "Some women notice feeling hungrier or craving richer, sweeter foods in the days before their period, though this varies considerably between people and cycles — research finds a higher average energy intake in this phase, not a rule every mother will experience. Eating enough through the day, rather than restricting and then giving in, tends to make those cravings easier to meet without distress.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup) with banana and roasted almonds (15g), made with milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana masala (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-onion curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Dark chocolate (20g) with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small slice of banana bread with a glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Steady habits over strict syncing", "notes": "You may come across meal plans built entirely around your cycle''s phases. The underlying idea that hormones affect appetite is reasonable, but rigid phase-by-phase eating rules aren''t well supported by evidence. Regular meals with enough protein, fat, fibre and key minerals serve most cycle-related needs better than a strict schedule.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with jowar roti (2 pieces) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a leafy green vegetable", "lunch_b": "Prawn-and-drumstick curry (100g prawns) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a matki curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "If cramps or heavy flow make eating harder", "notes": "Cramping, nausea or low appetite around your period can make regular meals feel like more effort than usual. Smaller, simpler meals eaten more often, warm foods and fluids, and not forcing a full plate when you feel unwell are all reasonable adjustments — they don''t undo anything about how you''ve been eating the rest of the month. Worth knowing: Bleeding through one or more pads or tampons every hour for several hours, feeling faint or markedly dizzy, experiencing severe or worsening pain, or having difficulty staying upright requires urgent medical assessment. Bleeding lasting more than seven days, repeated large clots, increasing fatigue or a major change from your previous pattern should also be discussed promptly with your healthcare provider.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (2) with vegetable toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "A hearty chicken-and-vegetable soup (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "Warm milk (200ml) with a pinch of turmeric", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted makhana (15g) with a banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, dal about a third of that) with ghee and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A few plain crackers with warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. No forced choices today. Has anything about your appetite, energy or cravings shifted since your cycle changed this year — and have you been treating that shift as information, or as something to fight?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (1 medium) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion omelette (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Seasonal fruit with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk (200ml) with roasted cumin and roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to meet a hungrier week with more food instead of less — trusting that your appetite knows something useful, even when it''s inconvenient?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: regular, adequate meals serve you better than rules built around any single number on a calendar or a scale.')
